========================================
        EXPENSE TRACKER ROADMAP
========================================

TECH STACK
----------
Frontend  : React + Tailwind CSS
Backend   : Node.js + Express.js
Database  : MySQL


========================================
PHASE 1 — MYSQL DATABASE
========================================

GOAL:
Build and understand the database independently.

TASKS:
1. Create database
2. Create expenses table
3. Understand data types
4. Primary Key
5. AUTO_INCREMENT
6. NOT NULL
7. DEFAULT
8. INSERT
9. SELECT
10. WHERE
11. UPDATE
12. DELETE
13. ORDER BY
14. LIMIT

TABLE:

expenses
---------
id
title
amount
category
description
expense_date
created_at

PRACTICE:
- Insert multiple expenses
- Display all expenses
- Find expense by id
- Find expenses by category
- Update an expense
- Delete an expense
- Sort expenses
- Limit results

DONE WHEN:
I can comfortably perform CRUD operations using SQL.


========================================
PHASE 2 — NODE.JS + EXPRESS
========================================

GOAL:
Build a basic backend server and understand REST APIs.

TASKS:
1. Create Node project
2. package.json
3. Install Express
4. Create server
5. app.listen()
6. GET route
7. POST route
8. PUT route
9. DELETE route
10. req.params
11. req.query
12. req.body
13. res.json()
14. res.status()
15. express.json()
16. Middleware
17. CORS

API STRUCTURE:

GET    /api/expenses
GET    /api/expenses/:id
POST   /api/expenses
PUT    /api/expenses/:id
DELETE /api/expenses/:id

PRACTICE:
- Create basic routes
- Send JSON responses
- Receive data from requests
- Test APIs using Postman

DONE WHEN:
I understand how an Express API receives requests
and sends responses.


========================================
PHASE 3 — NODE.JS + MYSQL
========================================

GOAL:
Connect the backend to the database and build
a complete CRUD API.

TASKS:
1. Install mysql2
2. Connect Node to MySQL
3. Create connection pool
4. Execute SQL from Node
5. GET expenses from MySQL
6. INSERT expense
7. UPDATE expense
8. DELETE expense
9. Prepared statements
10. Error handling
11. dotenv
12. Environment variables

FLOW:

Express
   ↓
MySQL Query
   ↓
MySQL Database
   ↓
Result
   ↓
Express
   ↓
JSON Response

PRACTICE:
- GET all expenses
- GET expense by ID
- POST expense
- PUT expense
- DELETE expense
- Test everything in Postman

DONE WHEN:
The entire Expense Tracker backend works
without React.


========================================
PHASE 4 — REACT + TAILWIND CSS
========================================

GOAL:
Build the frontend UI independently.

TASKS:
1. React project setup
2. Components
3. JSX
4. useState
5. useEffect
6. Forms
7. Form inputs
8. Event handling
9. map()
10. Conditional rendering
11. Tailwind CSS
12. Flexbox
13. Grid
14. Responsive design

COMPONENTS:

App
├── Navbar
├── ExpenseForm
├── ExpenseList
│   └── ExpenseCard
├── FilterBar
└── ExpenseSummary

UI:

- Navbar
- Add Expense form
- Expense cards
- Edit button
- Delete button
- Category dropdown
- Total spending section

FIRST:
Use dummy data.

DONE WHEN:
The frontend looks and behaves correctly
using dummy data without the backend.


========================================
PHASE 5 — CONNECT FRONTEND + BACKEND
========================================

GOAL:
Turn the separate frontend and backend into
one complete full-stack application.

FLOW:

React
  ↓
fetch()
  ↓
Express API
  ↓
MySQL
  ↓
Express
  ↓
JSON
  ↓
React


TASKS:
1. GET expenses
2. Display database data
3. POST new expense
4. Update UI after adding
5. DELETE expense
6. Edit expense
7. PUT updated expense
8. Loading state
9. Error handling
10. Empty state

PRACTICE:

Add expense
    ↓
React
    ↓
POST request
    ↓
Express
    ↓
MySQL
    ↓
Database updated
    ↓
React displays new expense


DONE WHEN:
I can add, view, edit and delete expenses
through the React UI and the changes are
actually stored in MySQL.


========================================
PHASE 6 — FEATURES + POLISH
========================================

GOAL:
Make the project portfolio-ready.

FEATURES:

1. Category filtering
2. Search expenses
3. Total spending
4. Spending by category
5. GROUP BY
6. SUM()
7. COUNT()
8. Dashboard statistics
9. Edit modal
10. Pagination
11. Better error messages
12. Loading indicators
13. Responsive design
14. Form validation
15. .env
16. .gitignore
17. GitHub
18. Deployment


FINAL FLOW:

              EXPENSE TRACKER
                     |
          ┌──────────┴──────────┐
          ↓                     ↓
      FRONTEND               BACKEND
          ↓                     ↓
 React + Tailwind       Node.js + Express
                                |
                                ↓
                              MySQL
                                |
                                ↓
                         Expenses Table


FINAL PROJECT SHOULD SUPPORT:

✓ Add expense
✓ View expenses
✓ Edit expense
✓ Delete expense
✓ Search
✓ Filter
✓ Total spending
✓ Category statistics
✓ Responsive UI
✓ REST API
✓ MySQL database
✓ Error handling
✓ Environment variables


